        "summary": "Operational conditions in the Strait of Hormuz remain severely disrupted. Port arrivals across five Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides totalled 469 in the most recent 24 hours, while PortWatch's most recent published day (2026-08-09, reflecting the outlet's weekly Tuesday release schedule) recorded 1 transit against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; the two figures use different methodologies and should not be equated. At 11:53 UTC, 324 vessels were holding position away from berth, excluding ships within 25 kilometres of a working port. Brent stood at $87.38 at 11:53 UTC, down 1.75% over 24 hours; WTI was at $81.65. The site's Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band and is rising, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast composite is in the elevated band and easing, with Polymarket escalation contracts as the top contributor; the two indices diverge, measuring present conditions and forward probability separately. On the incidents front, newstodaynet.com reports Iranian-backed Houthis killed six people in a vessel attack, a claim not independently confirmed. Signalscv.com separately reports US forces disabled an Iran-bound ship using Hellfire missiles, unverified and single-source. Pakistan is attempting to bring the United States and Iran back to dialogue as ship attacks continue, per morningstaronline.co.uk, while ibtimes.co.uk reports a Revolutionary Guard adviser warned of military operations on US soil after peace talks collapsed. Eleven events were indexed in the past 24 hours.",
